maria: Quick check — PR #842 failing in Next Edge build only. Local Node dev works. Error at build: Error [ERR_REQUIRE_ESM]: Must use import to load ES Module. Stack snippet: `Error: require() of ES module ...`

sam: Saw that before. Our SDK has `type:"module"` in package.json so CJS consumers break. Edge runtimes prefer ESM; bundlers sometimes choke. Which SDK version?

maria: @sam using @redwood/sdk v1.4.2 (published). Works in serverless lambdas but Next Edge fails at build: `Module parse failed: Unexpected token 'export'`.

kevin: Could be exports/main mapping. Check package.json `exports` and `main`/`module` fields. Recommended pattern:
```
"exports": {
  "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
  "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs"
}
```
Also ship both .mjs and .cjs builds.

yun: We solved this by adding an `edge`-friendly ESM entrypoint that avoids node-only imports. Avoid top-level `require('fs')` or `require('crypto')`. Lazy import node-only modules inside NODE runtime paths.

sam: Another gotcha: Vite/Next will try to polyfill node builtins and fail with "Can't resolve 'crypto'". For edge you need to rely on Web Crypto or provide an explicit shim. Don't let bundler pull in node shims.

maria: I tried aliasing `crypto` to an empty module in Vite but then runtime needs WebCrypto and fails. Any recommended shim strategy?

kevin: Prefer this approach:
1) Make an ESM edge build that uses `globalThis.crypto.subtle` where available.
2) In the source, wrap node-only code:
```
export async function computeHash(input){
  if (typeof globalThis.crypto?.subtle !== 'undefined') {
    const data = new TextEncoder().encode(input);
    return crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', data);
  }
  // node fallback
  const { createHash } = await import('crypto');
  return createHash('sha256').update(input).digest();
}
```
This avoids bundling node modules into the edge bundle.

yun: Also verify with `next build` in CI that the edge-targeted pages build; add a smoke test for edge runtime. For immediate unblock, import the ESM path directly: `await import('@redwood/sdk/dist/esm')` or add a dedicated `@redwood/sdk/edge` export.

maria: I'll push a small change to PR #842 to lazy-import the SDK in the edge page and add a fallback. Ping me for review once CI passes. I'll also add a short note in SDK README about edge usage and the recommended exports map.

dev-bot: Label `devex/edge` added to PR #842; reviewers requested: @sam, @kevin.

sam: :thumbsup: I'll take a look after tests finish.

kevin: I can draft the exports mapping and a small build script to emit .mjs for edge if you want.

maria: Thanks — that'd be great. I'll share the PR link when ready.
